#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ba94d7-362e-54af-a4dc-5650b2efcf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{csc}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386102} {:answer/id #uuid "0100602d-f98b-513d-9cb0-94a20d3703e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386103} {:answer/id #uuid "ea707e80-3f6b-5ca4-a2d4-29097932496c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386104} {:answer/id #uuid "4278f499-f5e5-5ac4-b8e0-fc854a2288e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386105} {:answer/id #uuid "028fc07b-3f60-56d1-8edb-1fb1620551ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "028fc07b-3f60-56d1-8edb-1fb1620551ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b18467-8af9-5ad0-97da-81cd55579f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c12e390-7cac-5d15-8cb3-07a2b192a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-104533" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5126c3d8-978f-582d-85b3-1b273093c176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556615} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba1a2b2-1160-53df-9d34-b83861b916c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556764} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b2a2c-fb0b-5db0-9b18-977f28ffedf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556874} {:answer/id #uuid "d151476d-b4bb-5405-af19-fe054e71c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} {:answer/id #uuid "d70bd935-3591-5e22-b322-ce29e2d33415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d151476d-b4bb-5405-af19-fe054e71c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fdecf4-7be9-5050-b772-01b00e36fa6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "325cb3ef-db56-5f90-98ae-212e45702d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109145" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93561cb9-31e8-5f50-b3bf-ac1e4c078018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430018} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7a244a-9ef8-56e4-ad31-1e3ad490a401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430020} {:answer/id #uuid "b0993602-e32e-5385-8615-215b677568b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430023} {:answer/id #uuid "c21c3926-fbd8-543d-9a89-059162436863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430024} {:answer/id #uuid "c6975ddc-3a08-5688-b3d1-0de4aad419be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6975ddc-3a08-5688-b3d1-0de4aad419be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd353f17-4f1a-5904-b62b-8c4277a7e856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5eff5095-a0c0-5448-9e3d-e45ec11b1792" :question/math-academy-id "q-109186" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a70a12201e99ded8eed85b797c760862814e1f0a50129020fa6d3b35d4334394.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts (see figure above). What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e914b0d-41ca-50e1-920b-daa562691bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556570} {:answer/id #uuid "772b2fe0-64e6-5c8e-a51d-7d63a2be61f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556701} {:answer/id #uuid "bacbe449-5ccd-5971-8f8e-92d50f996dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556886} {:answer/id #uuid "d807f0fc-fb4a-570e-8af5-96a9583d0e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556964} {:answer/id #uuid "f181cf37-c4e6-5144-a534-9ebcde59a058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bacbe449-5ccd-5971-8f8e-92d50f996dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556886} :answer-field/id #uuid "54638d73-c9f4-5d31-93a7-16d80e13c507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbe7ea72-e04a-55e6-a4f7-365b15606888" :question/math-academy-id "q-109257" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-1$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi [1-(-1)] \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422bd46c-effb-5d0b-904d-3ee35544d973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430009} {:answer/id #uuid "53183f8e-14d6-52c2-9f1a-70be683a7846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430011} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} {:answer/id #uuid "a548f782-4430-54c1-9ade-29c1fd806b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430021} {:answer/id #uuid "c743a931-9156-5119-af96-2e6b26ebadfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6cbf3-7b66-5401-9a6d-5259b70ef31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e501ffe3-f914-5273-b8d8-73d2e5bc3adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109269" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})=\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}(3-x)}{3}(\\frac{x+1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-x)(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3+2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {[3x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
